    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been looking at the gradient activity, but there's a weird issue—the \"specialized\" layers that handle the topological phase heuristics seem to have such high entanglement with the baseline physics weights that I can't seem to isolate them. It feels like any pruning I do to satisfy the reviewers is going to basically lobotomize the model's ability to actually recognize the state we're trying to prove exists. If I strip these layers, the model might just give garbage results for the reproducibility test. How can we handle this without losing the core predictive capability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
